WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.010
Hi everyone, it's Jerry. The Rubinstein Trap
is a trap for white out of the Queen's Gambit

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:17.240
Declined. How it works exactly is to have
these three queenside pieces eventually converging

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:24.240
on the c7 square. Here's how it works. After
d4, d5, c4, e6...the Queen's Gambit Declined.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:30.970
We have now what follows, a group of developing
moves... a group of logical moves for both

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:36.870
sides. Or in other words...minor pieces getting
out, getting castled, getting rooks to half-opened

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:43.870
files. So knight c3, knight f6. White pins
the knight, black breaks the pin. e3, black

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:51.500
castles, and after a couple knight moves we
have a very common position. One that could

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:58.360
come about from a different move order. And
before bishop d3 is played, the tension between

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:05.360
these two pawns is first resolved. So c takes
d, e takes d, and only then bishop d3. Rook

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:12.170
to e8, logical enough...half-opened file,
and white doing similar along the c-file.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:17.790
Sensing pressure against the c7 pawn, c6 is
very common...ruling out any possibilities

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:23.520
for knight b5 to maybe converge on the c7
square, as well as just reinforcing the center

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.520
on d5. After now castles we have h6, throwing
a question to the bishop, and more long term

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.820
providing a flight square for the king even
though at the moment it is being guarded.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:43.820
After bishop f4, the trap has been set. And
it's one that can very easily catch black,

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:52.240
the side who may very well be aware of this
idea of the bishop pair being very good in

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:57.710
a chess game. Or in other words, trying to
get a knight for bishop exchange. In this

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:04.020
instance here, it's faulty. Running after
this bishop pair in this case right here with

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:10.290
knight to h5, looking to get that dark-squared
bishop, runs into the tactic of knight takes

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:16.050
on d5. Knowing full well that if pawn takes
knight, bishop c7, the queen is dead. She

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:22.450
has no squares. Black can of course bail out
at this moment right here and not take the

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:28.000
knight. And instead knight takes f4, but note
that the knight can sidestep the pawn's threat,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.840
and just recapture on f4. And after the smoke
has cleared, white is up a pawn, and a very

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:40.730
significant one at that being that it is a
central pawn, and white clearly stands better.

